🚽 Test case: #197 -- assigning a wrong-tag pointer into a pointer-typed
🚽 struct FIELD (`a.next = &r;` where `next` is `gang Node *` and `r` is a
🚽 `gang Rect`) is rejected, closing the store path that #197's chained
🚽 read `a.next.val` now depends on. Without this, a Rect* planted in a
🚽 Node* field would later be read back through Node's layout by
🚽 `a.next.val` -- for differently-sized structs, an out-of-bounds access.
🚽 The guard is the same struct-tag assignment check added for #196
🚽 (semantic_visit_assignment), here exercised with a struct-field target
🚽 rather than a plain pointer variable.
gang Node {
    rizz val;
    gang Node *next;
};
gang Rect {
    rizz w;
    rizz h;
};

skibidi main {
    gang Node a;
    gang Rect r;
    a.next = &r;
    yapping("%d", a.next.val);
}
